---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="QuiltClarity Calculation Methodology and Defaults"
  description="Review QuiltClarity's units, seam and usable-width rules, purchase rounding, calculator formulas, optimizer priorities, and test approach."
>
  <article class="page article-page">
    <header class="hero compact-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
      <h1>How QuiltClarity calculates results</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Every displayed recommendation comes from explicit inputs, shared domain
        rules, and deterministic calculations that can be checked independently.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="article-body">
      <section>
        <h2>Units and dimensions</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            All calculations use millimetres internally with exact conversion
            constants.
          </li>
          <li>
            Usable WOF means fabric remaining after selvages; the editable
            default is 40 inches.
          </li>
          <li>
            Finished rectangle dimensions gain seam allowance on both opposite
            edges. Cut dimensions do not.
          </li>
          <li>
            Piece-level orientation and rotation choices override fabric
            defaults.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Fabric recommendations</h2>
        <p>
          Calculators first determine the layout or construction length, then
          add the selected safety allowance. Purchase length always rounds
          upward to the selected shop increment—never to the nearest increment
          or down.
        </p>
        <p>
          Backing compares every valid panel direction and reports the
          lowest-yardage candidate without claiming it is always aesthetically
          preferable. Borders and sashing deduct fabric consumed by joins before
          counting WOF capacity.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Cutting planner priorities</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>
            Produce a valid plan that accounts for every requested piece exactly
            once.
          </li>
          <li>Prefer practical strip-based cutting.</li>
          <li>Prefer less fabric length, then lower cut complexity.</li>
          <li>
            Use stable tie-breaks so identical inputs produce identical output.
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Large valid inputs use a simpler grouped heuristic with a visible
          warning so browser work stays bounded. The diagram projects the chosen
          optimizer placements; it does not calculate a second layout.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Testing and source conventions</h2>
        <p>
          Automated golden fixtures and invariants cover conversions, placement
          bounds, non-overlap, orientation, purchase bounds, determinism,
          backing coverage, HST yields, and joined-strip capacity. Conventions
          that vary in practice remain editable and are described as defaults.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <a
              href="https://help.prequilt.com/prequilt-101/fabric-calculator/calculations-explained/fabric-cutting-diagrams/"
              >PreQuilt cutting-diagram explanation</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://quiltkeeperstudio.com/calculators/backing"
              >QuiltKeeper backing conventions</a
            >
          </li>
          <li>
            <a href="https://haileystitches.com/half-square-triangle-charts/"
              >Hailey Stitches HST method reference</a
            >
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          These practical references inform convention review; QuiltClarity's
          locked, test-backed calculation contracts control the actual
          implementation.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
